      "text": "To solve for the distance between the vertices of the given hyperbola \\(\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\\), we start by identifying the standard form of the hyperbola equation and the parameters involved.\n\nThe given equation is:\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\n\\]\n\nThis equation is in the standard form of a hyperbola that opens vertically (up and down):\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nFrom this form, we can directly read off the values of \\(a^2\\) and \\(b^2\\):\n\\[\na^2 = 27 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 = 11\n\\]\n\nNext, we find \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\\[\na = \\sqrt{27} = 3\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\sqrt{11}\n\\]\n\nFor a hyperbola of the form \\(\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\\), the vertices are located at \\((0, \\pm a)\\). Therefore, the vertices of this hyperbola are at:\n\\[\n(0, \\pm 3\\sqrt{3})\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the vertices is the distance between the points \\((0, 3\\sqrt{3})\\) and \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3})\\). This distance is simply the difference in the \\(y\\)-coordinates of the vertices:\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = 3\\sqrt{3} - (-3\\sqrt{3}) = 3\\sqrt{3} + 3\\sqrt{3} = 6\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]",
